1. In 1976, during peak demand periods, approximately 400 tree fruit workers in total were required in the Creston,
B.C. area. By crop they were:	cherries 70; pears 50; prunes 30;		
apples 250. All peaches themselves.	were picked	by	the growers2.	Workers requested	(a) Met	(b) Not MetMay 10-14	8	8	0May 17-21	3	3	0May 24-28	0	0	0May 31-June 4	0	0	0June 7-11	8	8	0June 14-18	15	15	0June 21-25	3	3	0June 28-July 2	17	17	0July 5-9	22	22	0July 12-16	27	25	2July 19-23	33	33	0July 26-31	22	22	0August 2-6	12	12	02.	Workers requested	(a) Met	(b) Not MetAugust 9-13	4	4	0August 16-20	4	4	0August 23-27	0	0	0August 30-September 3	10	10	0September 6-10	10	10	0September 13-17	9	9	0September 20-24	38	38	0September 27-October 1	68	68	0October 4-8	74	74	0October 12-15	32	32	0October 18-22	41	41	0October 25-29	7	7	0Totals	467	465	23.	Fifty workers.4.	Of a total of 190 tree fruit growers in the Creston area: (a) 18; (b) 4; (c) 168.5.	Additional accommodation is required for approximately 100 workers.6.	Extensive efforts were made by the Creston Farm Labour Office to recruit Creston area residents as fruit workers. Communication was maintained with local secondary schools; posters advising of jobs available were placed in local restaurants, public notice boards, bus depot, etc.; a farm labour bulletin was distributed to all news media on a weekly basis; local radio advertising was used when required; full co-operation existed between Canada Manpower Centres and Unemployment Insurance Commission and British Columbia Department of Human Resources during the harvest season.
